In C++, a pointer is a variable that holds the memory address of another variable. A reference, in contrast, is an alias for a variable, meaning it refers to the same memory location as the original variable. Both pointers and references are significant in C++ programming, but they serve different purposes and have distinct use cases.

Pointers in C++:

To declare a pointer in C++, the asterisk symbol (\*) is used before the pointer name. The asterisk indicates that the variable is a pointer, and the variable name specifies the type of data the pointer is pointing to. For example:
```c++
int* p;
```
This declaration creates a pointer named p that can store the memory address of an integer variable. The memory address of an integer variable can be assigned to the pointer using the address-of operator (&). For instance:
```c++
int x = 10;
int* p = &x;
```
This code declares an integer variable x and initializes it to 10. A pointer p is then declared and assigned the memory address of x using the & operator.

Pointers can also be used for dynamic memory allocation on the heap. For example:
```c++
int* p = new int;
*p = 10;
```
This code allocates memory on the heap for an integer and assigns the value 10 to it. The memory location can be accessed using the pointer p.

Pointers are useful for creating arrays dynamically on the heap. For example:
```c++
int* arr = new int[5];
```
This code creates an array of five integers on the heap and assigns the memory address of the first element to the pointer arr.

Pointers are also utilized in function parameters, allowing variables to be passed by reference or by value. For example:
```c++
void swap(int* a, int* b)
{
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

int main()
{
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    swap(&x, &y);
    // x is now 20, y is now 10
    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a swap function that takes two integer pointers as parameters and swaps their values. The swap function is called with the memory addresses of x and y as arguments.

References in C++:

To declare a reference in C++, the ampersand symbol (&) is used before the reference name. For example:
```c++
int x = 10;
int& ref = x;
```
This code declares a reference named ref and initializes it to the variable x. It is important to note that ref is not a pointer; it is an alias for x. Any changes made to ref will also affect x, and vice versa.

References are often used in function parameters to pass variables by reference. For example:
```c++
void swap(int& a, int& b)
{
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int main()
{
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    swap(x, y);
    // x is now 20, y is now 10
    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a swap function that takes two integer references as parameters and swaps their values. The swap function is called with x and y as arguments.

A notable difference between pointers and references is that pointers can be reassigned to point to different memory locations, whereas references cannot be reassigned. For example:
```c++
int x = 10, y = 20;
int* p = &x;
p = &y; // p now points to y
```

In C++, a union is a special data type that allows different data types to be stored in the same memory location. Unions share similarities with structures in that they can contain multiple members, but they differ in that only one member can be accessed at a time. Here's an example:

```c++
#include <iostream>

union MyUnion {
    int i;
    char c;
    double d;
};

int main() {
    MyUnion u;
    u.i = 10;
    std::cout << "Value of i: " << u.i << "\n";
    u.c = 'a';
    std::cout << "Value of c: " << u.c << "\n";
    u.d = 3.14;
    std::cout << "Value of d: " << u.d << "\n";
    std::cout << "Value of i: " << u.i << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, a union named MyUnion is defined with three members of different types: an integer (i), a character (c), and a double (d). Only one member of the union can be accessed at a time, and modifying one member affects the values of the others.

Anonymous unions in C++ are unions without a name. They are defined inside a struct or class, allowing their members to be accessed directly without using the union name. Here's an example:

```c++
#include <iostream>

struct MyStruct {
    union {
        int i;
        char c;
        double d;
    };
};

