    std::cout << "Value of d: " << s.d << "\n";
    std::cout << "Value of i: " << s.i << "\n";
    return 0;
}
```

In this example, a struct named MyStruct contains an anonymous union. This allows direct access to the union's members without needing a union name.

A union in C++ is a special data type that allows different data types to be stored in the same memory location. It shares similarities with a struct, but a key distinction is that only one member of a union can be accessed at any given time. Here is an illustrative example:

