In this example, a struct named MyStruct contains an anonymous union with three members. The members can be accessed directly, as if they were part of the struct itself.

The primary difference between a union and a struct is that a union can hold only one value at a time, whereas a struct can hold multiple values simultaneously. Changing a union member affects the others, while changing a struct member does not impact the other members.

In summary, unions provide a way to store different data types in the same memory location, with only one member accessible at a time. Anonymous unions are defined within a struct or class, allowing direct access to their members. Reasonable people may have different views on when to use unions versus structs, and it is important to consider the specific requirements of a program when making this choice.

