In JavaScript, one way to find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters is demonstrated in the following example:

```csharp
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  let charMap = {};
  let start = 0;
  let maxLength = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    let char = s[i];
    if (charMap[char] >= start) {
      start = charMap[char] + 1;
    }
    charMap[char] = i;
    maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, i - start + 1);
  }

  return maxLength;
}
```

This algorithm employs a sliding window technique to manage the current substring and its unique characters. It utilizes a hash map (`charMap`) to record the last position of each character within the substring. The variable `start` tracks the starting position of the current substring, while `maxLength` maintains the length of the longest substring identified so far. For each character in the input string, the algorithm checks if the character has already appeared in the current substring. If it has, the starting position of the substring is adjusted to the last position of the duplicate character plus one. The character's position is then updated in the hash map, and the length of the current substring is calculated and compared to the maximum length found so far. The final output is the length of the longest substring without repeating characters.

