```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> partition(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {
    std::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k, arr.end());
    return std::vector<int>(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k);
}
```

This function takes in a reference to a `std::vector` of integers and an integer `k`, and returns a new `std::vector` containing the first `k` elements of the input vector, in the order that they appear in the input vector. The `std::nth_element()` function is used to partially sort the input vector so that the element at the kth position is in its final sorted position, with all elements before it being less than or equal to it, and all elements after it being greater than or equal to it.

It is important to note that while the `numpy.partition()` function in Python returns a tuple of two arrays, this C++ function returns a single vector containing the first k elements in the input vector.

