}
```

In this approach, `std::nth_element()` is applied to partially sort the input vector, ensuring that the element at the kth position is in its final sorted position. This means all elements before it are less than or equal to it, and all elements after it are greater than or equal to it. Following this, `std::partial_sort()` is used to sort the first k elements of the input vector. Supporters of this method suggest it is more efficient than sorting the entire vector, as it focuses only on the first `k` elements, leaving the rest in an unspecified order, which can be faster than sorting the entire array.

The following C++ function is designed to be more memory efficient by sorting the input vector in place and returning a subrange of the sorted input vector without allocating a new vector:

```cpp
