A generic `CommandDispatcher` in C# serves as a mechanism to direct commands or actions to their respective handlers without needing to create specific dispatchers for each command type. The concept involves having a dispatcher capable of handling any command type and routing it to the appropriate handler based on the command's type.

To implement a generic `CommandDispatcher`, one can use interfaces, generics, and dependency injection. The `ICommandHandler<TCommand>` interface can define a handler for a specific command type, where `TCommand` represents the command type.

For instance, consider two command types, `AddCustomerCommand` and `DeleteCustomerCommand`. Handlers for these commands can be defined as follows:
```csharp
public interface ICommandHandler<TCommand>
{
    Task HandleAsync(TCommand command);
}

public class AddCustomerCommandHandler : ICommandHandler<AddCustomerCommand>
{
    public async Task HandleAsync(AddCustomerCommand command)
    {
        // Handle the AddCustomerCommand
    }
}

public class DeleteCustomerCommandHandler : ICommandHandler<DeleteCustomerCommand>
{
    public async Task HandleAsync(DeleteCustomerCommand command)
    {
        // Handle the DeleteCustomerCommand
    }
}
```
The `CommandDispatcher` class can be defined as a generic class that accepts a type parameter `TCommand` and uses dependency injection to resolve the appropriate handler for that command type:
```csharp
public class CommandDispatcher<TCommand> where TCommand : class
{
    private readonly IServiceProvider _serviceProvider;

    public CommandDispatcher(IServiceProvider serviceProvider)
    {
        _serviceProvider = serviceProvider;
    }

    public async Task DispatchAsync(TCommand command)
    {
        var handler = _serviceProvider.GetService(typeof(ICommandHandler<>).MakeGenericType(command.GetType()));

        if (handler == null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException($"No handler registered for command of type {command.GetType().FullName}");
        }

        await ((dynamic)handler).HandleAsync((dynamic)command);
    }
}
```
In this setup, the `DispatchAsync` method accepts a command object of type `TCommand` and resolves the appropriate `ICommandHandler<TCommand>` implementation using dependency injection. It then invokes the `HandleAsync` method on the handler to process the command.

To utilize the `CommandDispatcher`, command handlers and the `CommandDispatcher` can be registered with a dependency injection container as follows:
```swift
var services = new ServiceCollection();

services.AddTransient<ICommandHandler<AddCustomerCommand>, AddCustomerCommandHandler>();
services.AddTransient<ICommandHandler<DeleteCustomerCommand>, DeleteCustomerCommandHandler>();
services.AddTransient(typeof(CommandDispatcher<>));

var serviceProvider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
```
Finally, the `CommandDispatcher` can be used to dispatch commands in this manner:
```swift
var commandDispatcher = serviceProvider.GetService<CommandDispatcher<AddCustomerCommand>>();

await commandDispatcher.DispatchAsync(new AddCustomerCommand());
```
This approach dispatches an `AddCustomerCommand` to the corresponding `AddCustomerCommandHandler` for processing.

