To set the `href` and `download` attributes of an anchor (a) element using jQuery, you can use the `attr` method. Here's an example:

```php
<a id="downloadCSV" href="#">Download CSV</a>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#downloadCSV').click(function() {
    // Create a CSV data string
    var csvData = "Name,Email\nJohn Doe,john.doe@example.com\nJane Smith,jane.smith@example.com";

    // Encode the CSV data as a data URI
    var csvUrl = "data:text/csv;charset=utf-8," + encodeURIComponent(csvData);

    // Set the href and download attributes of the anchor element
    $(this).attr({
      'href': csvUrl,
      'download': 'data.csv'
    });
  });
});
</script>
```

In this example, clicking on the anchor element with ID `downloadCSV` will create a CSV data string, encode it as a data URI, and set the `href` and `download` attributes of the anchor element to the data URI and file name respectively.

The `attr` method takes an object with the attribute names and values as key-value pairs. In this case, the `href` and `download` attributes of the anchor element are set using the `attr` method.

The `download` attribute specifies the name of the file to be downloaded. In this example, it is set to "data.csv", but this can be updated as desired.

It is important to note that not all browsers support the `download` attribute, and some may not support the `data` URI scheme. Users are encouraged to consider providing a fallback option for browsers that do not support these features.

To set the `href` and `download` attributes of an anchor (a) element one by one using jQuery chaining, you can use the `attr` method with chaining. Here's an example:

```php
