```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <utility>

template <typename K, typename V>
class HashMap {
private:
    static const int ARRAY_SIZE = 101;  // Prime number
    std::list<std::pair<K, V>> table[ARRAY_SIZE];

    int hash(const K& key) const {
        // Hash function for string keys
        int hash = 0;
        for (char c : key) {
            hash = (hash * 31 + c) % ARRAY_SIZE;
        }
        return hash;
    }

public:
    void put(const K& key, const V& value) {
        int index = hash(key);
        for (auto& pair : table[index]) {
            if (pair.first == key) {
                pair.second = value;
                return;
            }
        }
        table[index].emplace_back(key, value);
    }

    V get(const K& key) const {
        int index = hash(key);
        for (const auto& pair : table[index]) {
            if (pair.first == key) {
                return pair.second;
            }
        }
        throw std::out_of_range("Key not found.");
    }

    void remove(const K& key) {
        int index = hash(key);
        auto it = table[index].begin();
        while (it != table[index].end()) {
            if (it->first == key) {
                it = table[index].erase(it);
                return;
            }
            ++it;
        }
        throw std::out_of_range("Key not found.");
    }
};
```

In this implementation, the `HashMap` class template uses two type parameters `K` and `V` for the key and value types, respectively. The `table` member variable is an array of linked lists, where each element in the array serves as a head node of a linked list that stores key-value pairs with the same hash code.

The `hash()` function is a basic hash function that processes a string key and returns a hash code as an integer between 0 and `ARRAY_SIZE - 1`.

The `put()` method is designed to insert a key-value pair into the hash map. It calculates the hash code for the key using the `hash()` function and then checks the linked list at the corresponding index to determine if the key already exists. If it does, the value associated with the key is updated. If not, a new node with the key-value pair is created and added to the end of the linked list.

The `get()` method retrieves a value from the hash map. It calculates the hash code for the key using the `hash()` function and then searches the linked list at the corresponding index for the node with the matching key. If the key is found, the corresponding value is returned. If the key is not found, an exception is thrown.

The `remove()` method deletes a key-value pair from the hash map. It calculates the hash code for the key using the `hash()` function and then searches the linked list at the corresponding index for the node with the matching key. If the key is found, the corresponding node is removed from the linked list. If the key is not found, an exception is thrown.

It is important to note that this is a straightforward implementation and may not be suitable for all use cases. For instance, it does not handle collisions in the most efficient manner and does not resize the array when the number of key-value pairs exceeds a certain threshold.

