`htons` is a function found in the `<arpa/inet.h>` header file in C that is used to convert a 16-bit value from host byte order to network byte order. This function is commonly utilized when dealing with Internet socket addresses, which require network byte order.

Byte order refers to the sequence in which bytes are arranged within a value. On many modern computers, values are stored in little-endian byte order, where the least significant byte (LSB) is stored at the lowest memory address. Conversely, network byte order is consistently big-endian, meaning the most significant byte (MSB) is stored at the lowest memory address.

For example:
```c
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    unsigned short host_port = 0x1234;  /* host byte order */
    unsigned short net_port;            /* network byte order */

    /* convert host byte order to network byte order */
    net_port = htons(host_port);

    printf("Host byte order: 0x%x\n", host_port);
    printf("Network byte order: 0x%x\n", net_port);

    return 0;
}
```
The output of the program would be:
```vbnet
Host byte order: 0x1234
Network byte order: 0x3412
```
If you have further questions about byte order or network programming, feel free to ask.

