One method to consider is creating a wrapper method that invokes the custom object creation function in the IL2CPP binary and returns a handle or reference to the newly created object. This handle or reference can then be used to interact with the object as necessary, and returned to the wrapper method for proper cleanup by the garbage collector.

Here's an example of how a wrapper method might be used to create objects in the IL2CPP binary and manage their memory:
```csharp
// Define a wrapper method for object creation
public static class ObjectWrapper {
    [DllImport("MyIL2CPP.dll")]
    private static extern IntPtr CreateMyObject();

    [DllImport("MyIL2CPP.dll")]
    private static extern void DestroyMyObject(IntPtr obj);

    public static MyObject Create() {
        // Call the custom object creation method in the IL2CPP binary
        IntPtr objPtr = CreateMyObject();

        // Convert the raw pointer to a managed object using a wrapper class
        MyObjectWrapper wrapper = new MyObjectWrapper(objPtr);
        return wrapper.Object;
    }

    public static void Destroy(MyObject obj) {
        // Convert the managed object to a raw pointer using the wrapper class
        MyObjectWrapper wrapper = new MyObjectWrapper(obj);
        IntPtr objPtr = wrapper.Ptr;

        // Call the custom object destruction method in the IL2CPP binary
        DestroyMyObject(objPtr);
    }

    private class MyObjectWrapper {
        public readonly MyObject Object;
        public readonly IntPtr Ptr;

        public MyObjectWrapper(MyObject obj) {
            Object = obj;
            GCHandle handle = GCHandle.Alloc(obj, GCHandleType.Pinned);
            Ptr = handle.AddrOfPinnedObject();
        }

        public MyObjectWrapper(IntPtr ptr) {
            Ptr = ptr;
            GCHandle handle = GCHandle.FromIntPtr(ptr);
            Object = (MyObject)handle.Target;
        }
    }
}

...

// Use the wrapper method to create and manage objects in the IL2CPP binary
MyObject obj = ObjectWrapper.Create();
// ...
ObjectWrapper.Destroy(obj);
```
In this example, `ObjectWrapper` is a static class providing `Create` and `Destroy` methods for object creation and memory management. The `Create` method uses P/Invoke to call the custom object creation method in the IL2CPP binary and returns a managed object that wraps the raw pointer. The `Destroy` method converts a managed object to a raw pointer using the wrapper class and calls the custom object destruction method in the IL2CPP binary using P/Invoke.

