The solution provided is considered efficient for datasets that are small to moderately sized. The conversion from the array to an object using `arrayToObject` has a time complexity of O(n), where n represents the number of elements in the array. Following this, the time complexity of the `getFieldOptions` function primarily depends on the number of options in `field_data`, which is generally small.

For those working with very large datasets, some suggest optimizing the `getFieldOptions` function by eliminating the conversion step and directly searching the array:
```javascript
function getFieldOptions(field_key) {
  const field = fieldsData.find((field) => field.field_key === field_key);

  if (!field || field.field_type !== "hysSelect") {
    return [];
  }

  return field.field_data.split(",").map((option) => {
    if (option === "") {
      return null;
    }
    return { label: option, value: option };
  }).filter(Boolean);
}
```
This method also has a time complexity of O(n) for searching the array. However, some point out that it might be slower than the previous solution if `getFieldOptions` is called frequently, as it searches the array each time. In such cases, others recommend considering caching the results or maintaining a separate object for lookup, as shown in the earlier approach.

